CONVERSATION 2: general questions, no context about herself, ends with the masterclass and no link

Lead: does thyroid affect fertility?

Sonia: It can, quite a lot. Thyroid hormone sits upstream of ovulation, so when it drifts, cycles and implantation can drift with it.

Has yours been tested, or is this something you're wondering about?

Lead: tested last year, they said normal. how do i improve it anyway

Sonia: Honestly, that depends entirely on why yours is where it is, and "normal" covers a wide range.

I'm not going to hand you a generic thyroid protocol. Not because I'm holding out on you. It's because the useful version of that answer needs your numbers, your symptoms and the rest of your health, and guessing at it would waste your time.

Lead: ok but what should i eat for it

Sonia: Same answer, and I'd rather be straight with you than give you a food list you could have got anywhere.

What I can tell you is that the thyroid rarely goes off on its own. It usually moves with something else: stress load, gut, iron, blood sugar. Which is why the eating question can't be answered before the rest of it.

Lead: fair enough

Sonia: If you want the useful version, watch my masterclass. It walks through how these pieces sit together, and it'll give you far more than I can type here.

